// Practice quiz: Modules, imports and virtual environments
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/modules-imports-and-virtual-environments

::o1 own module::Your script main.py sits next to utils.py, which defines slugify(). Which line imports just that function? {
~import utils.py #Not quite. Python reads utils.py as a submodule py of a package utils, so this raises ModuleNotFoundError.
~import slugify from utils #Not quite. That is JavaScript word order; in Python it is a SyntaxError.
~from utils.py import slugify #Not quite. The .py extension is never part of a module name.
=from utils import slugify #Correct. The module name is the file name without .py, and from ... import pulls out one name.
}

::o1 search path::Which statement best describes where Python looks for import pricing? {
=It searches sys.path in order; startup mode, environment and site configuration determine its entries #Correct. The durable rule is ordered sys.path lookup; the script directory or current directory, standard-library locations and site-packages are normal entries whose details vary.
~It always searches only the script folder, standard library and site-packages in that fixed order #Not quite. That is a useful normal-script sketch, but startup mode, PYTHONPATH and site configuration can change the entries.
~It searches the internet via pip #Not quite. import never downloads anything; pip installs packages beforehand.
~It searches every folder on the disk #Not quite. Only the entries in sys.path are searched.
}

::o1 missing package::pytest is installed in the project's .venv. You run python3 -m pytest with the venv not active and get No module named pytest. Why? {
~pytest must be imported with its version number #Not quite. Imports never include versions.
=The system python3 searches its own site-packages, which do not contain pytest #Correct. Each Python finds packages in its own site-packages; activate the venv or run .venv/bin/python.
~The requirements file is missing #Not quite. requirements.txt is not consulted at import time.
~pytest only works on Windows #Not quite. pytest runs on all major systems.
}

::o2 create a venv::Which command creates a virtual environment in the folder .venv on macOS or Linux? {
~pip install venv #Not quite. venv ships with Python; it is not installed with pip.
~python3 .venv #Not quite. This tries to run a folder as a script.
~source .venv/bin/activate #Not quite. That activates an environment that already exists; it does not create one.
=python3 -m venv .venv #Correct. venv is the standard-library module that creates environments.
}

::o2 activate on Windows::After py -m venv .venv, which pair uses the official activation command for each Windows shell? {
~cmd.exe: source .venv/bin/activate; PowerShell: source .venv/bin/activate #Not quite. source .venv/bin/activate is for POSIX shells, not these Windows shells.
~cmd.exe: .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1; PowerShell: .venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat #Not quite. The two shell-specific scripts are reversed.
=cmd.exe: .venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat; PowerShell: .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 #Correct. The venv activation table gives a different script for cmd.exe and PowerShell.
~cmd.exe: py activate .venv; PowerShell: python -m venv --activate #Not quite. Neither command is a venv activation command.
}

::o2 pinned install::With the venv active, which command installs exactly version 9.1.1 of pytest into it? {
~python -m pip install pytest 9.1.1 #Not quite. This asks for two packages, pytest and one named 9.1.1.
=python -m pip install pytest\=\=9.1.1 #Correct. \=\= pins the exact version, and python -m pip uses the pip of the active Python.
~python -m venv pytest\=\=9.1.1 #Not quite. venv creates environments; it does not install packages.
~import pytest\=\=9.1.1 #Not quite. import loads installed modules; it cannot install or pick versions.
}

::o3 record dependencies::Which command writes the packages installed in the active venv, in requirements format with exact versions, to requirements.txt? {
~python -m pip install -r requirements.txt #Not quite. That reads the file to install packages; it does not write it.
~python -m pip list requirements.txt #Not quite. list does not write a requirements file.
=python -m pip freeze, with its output redirected into requirements.txt #Correct. freeze prints one name\=\=version line per installed package (bootstrap tools such as pip are left out by default), and the shell redirect writes them to the file. It records what is installed; it is not a lockfile.
~git add .venv #Not quite. That would commit the environment, which should be ignored.
}

::o3 ignore the venv::Why is .venv listed in .gitignore instead of being committed? {
=It is large, tied to one machine's Python and paths, and can be rebuilt from requirements.txt #Correct. The venv documentation says environments are disposable, not portable, and not checked into source control.
~Git cannot store binary files #Not quite. Git can store binaries; the venv is excluded because it is not portable.
~It contains your source code #Not quite. Project code lives outside the venv.
~Python refuses to run a committed venv #Not quite. The reason is portability and size, not a Python rule.
}
